    "windows_exe": [
      "firefox.exe"
    ],
    "macos_bundle": [
      "org.mozilla.firefox"
    ],
    "window_title": []
  },
  "sources": [
    "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/keyboard-shortcuts-perform-firefox-tasks-quickly",
    "https://www-archive.mozilla.org/support/firefox/keyboard",
    "https://firefox-source-docs.mozilla.org/devtools-user/keyboard_shortcuts/index.html"
  ],
  "note": "The current SUMO article (first source URL, named in the brief) returned Mozilla's bot-verification \"Client Challenge\" interstitial to every fetch attempt from this environment, including via the Wayback Machine's own crawl of that URL, so it could not be read directly. Its content was instead sourced from Mozilla's own archived classic shortcuts reference (www-archive.mozilla.org, still an official Mozilla domain) for the core navigation/editing list, cross-checked against current behavior; a few shortcuts that page listed but modern Firefox has dropped (DOM Inspector, New Mail Message, the standalone search bar's Ctrl+Up/Down engine switcher) were left out, and Ctrl+H's Mac chord was corrected to Cmd+Shift+H (Cmd+H is reserved by macOS for Hide). \"Select tab [1 to 9]\" is expanded into 9 explicit actions. Reopen Closed Tab, New Private Window, Library, Bookmarks Toolbar toggle, Quit, Clear Recent History and the Add-ons Manager were confirmed current via Mozilla Support search results and added since the base list predates them. The Developer Tools section is complete from Mozilla's official devtools docs; its deeper per-pane editor shortcuts (Inspector's HTML/CSS pane navigation, Console autocomplete, Debugger source-editor bindings) were left out as internal editing minutiae rather than app-level commands. Generic single-key/OS text-editing entries the old list carried (Copy, Cut, Paste, Select All, Undo, Redo, Delete, plain arrow-key scrolling) were left out as not Firefox-specific. Skipped throughout as not expressible: mouse-only shortcuts.",
